WEBVTT

00:01.105 --> 00:02.862
This is my website today.

00:03.187 --> 00:06.105
There's a research map, a little butterfly,

00:06.330 --> 00:09.728
and even an assistant that answers questions about
my work.

00:10.233 --> 00:11.689
But it didn't start like this.

00:12.155 --> 00:15.092
Here's Version 1, from 2023.

00:15.558 --> 00:20.057
A name, a short profile, a menu, a few groups of
evidence.

00:20.382 --> 00:21.098
Pretty plain.

00:21.523 --> 00:23.600
And it started from a free template.

00:25.606 --> 00:28.663
Now here's what you often get if you just ask AI,

00:29.189 --> 00:33.328
"Make me a personal website." Welcome to my digital
universe.

00:33.633 --> 00:34.369
Innovator.

00:34.514 --> 00:35.209
Creator.

00:35.255 --> 00:36.023
Visionary.

00:36.023 --> 00:39.053
Fifty projects, ninety-eight percent success.

00:39.319 --> 00:40.655
Sounds impressive, right?

00:41.160 --> 00:43.257
The problem is, none of it is true.

00:43.563 --> 00:46.080
Nobody told AI who you are, so it guessed.

00:48.085 --> 00:50.562
So in this video, we're doing it differently.

00:50.848 --> 00:55.066
You'll start from a real project, give AI your real
facts,

00:55.332 --> 00:58.049
and approve the plan before it builds anything.

00:58.655 --> 01:01.832
By the end, you'll have Version 1 of your own
website.

01:02.278 --> 01:03.066
Not perfect.

01:03.066 --> 01:06.897
But true, and yours, and something you can open
again tomorrow.

01:07.703 --> 01:08.671
Hi, I'm Belle.

01:08.671 --> 01:10.340
Welcome to Structure Foundation.

01:12.385 --> 01:14.862
Everything in this video lives on one page.

01:15.168 --> 01:16.964
The link is right below the video.

01:17.489 --> 01:21.227
If it asks for an access code, use the one that came
with it.

01:21.633 --> 01:22.989
This is the lesson board.

01:22.994 --> 01:29.414
Four folders, left to right: Get Ready, Study
References, Build the Foundation, and Check and
Save.

01:29.919 --> 01:33.737
The numbers, like 00 to 06, are minutes from the
live workshop.

01:34.063 --> 01:35.359
You don't need to match them.

01:35.584 --> 01:36.940
They just show the order.

01:38.645 --> 01:45.072
Open any folder and you'll see the same three lines
at the top: Goal, Output, Checkpoint.

01:45.498 --> 01:47.496
If you ever feel lost, read those.

01:47.742 --> 01:49.520
They tell you what you're making right now.

01:51.626 --> 01:52.722
A few more things.

01:53.107 --> 01:56.426
Buttons that say Copy put text on your clipboard.

01:56.972 --> 01:59.549
Links with a little arrow open a new tab.

01:59.815 --> 02:03.694
At the bottom of each folder, Continue takes you to
the next one.

02:04.119 --> 02:06.257
This Lesson board link takes you back.

02:06.562 --> 02:09.260
And if things look tiny, hit Fit board.

02:11.006 --> 02:11.661
That's it.

02:11.987 --> 02:14.950
The video follows this board from left to right,

02:14.950 --> 02:17.873
so you can pause anytime and find where we are.

02:18.259 --> 02:20.979
Just watch the strip at the bottom of the screen.

02:23.245 --> 02:24.561
Okay, first folder.

02:24.887 --> 02:25.602
Get Ready.

02:26.248 --> 02:29.785
Up top, it says the final website is still HTML.

02:30.371 --> 02:34.649
HTML is just the page format your browser knows how
to show.

02:34.974 --> 02:38.552
And honestly, one AI-made HTML page can look great.

02:38.857 --> 02:40.654
Think of it like a finished cake.

02:40.879 --> 02:42.795
Sometimes a cake is all you need.

02:42.941 --> 02:45.838
But today we want the recipe and the baking kit too.

02:46.183 --> 02:48.740
Something we can come back to and keep improving.

02:50.746 --> 02:52.623
So here's how we split the work.

02:53.329 --> 02:56.668
AI handles the files, the code, the setup.

02:56.874 --> 03:01.474
You decide the facts, the direction, the boundaries,
and which version you keep.

03:01.940 --> 03:04.538
You won't touch a single technical file today.

03:04.864 --> 03:05.540
Promise.

03:07.625 --> 03:09.602
Next, open a Builder.

03:10.088 --> 03:14.486
If you already use Codex or Claude Code, great, use
that.

03:14.851 --> 03:16.848
They work right inside a project.

03:17.113 --> 03:20.531
If you don't have either, click Open Google AI
Studio.

03:20.816 --> 03:23.093
It's free, and it runs in your browser.

03:23.519 --> 03:27.176
Your result will look different from mine, and from
everyone else's.

03:27.442 --> 03:28.197
That's fine.

03:28.443 --> 03:31.500
We're practicing a process, not copying a page.

03:33.185 --> 03:34.722
Last thing: your CV.

03:35.208 --> 03:36.825
Make a public-safe copy.

03:37.211 --> 03:43.976
Take out your home address, your private phone
number, references, student data, anything
confidential.

03:44.302 --> 03:46.500
And grab one photo if you'd like.

03:54.606 --> 03:55.443
Welcome back.

03:55.689 --> 03:59.090
Click Continue, and you're in Study References.

03:59.517 --> 04:03.499
It's the biggest folder, so we'll go through it in
four parts.

04:05.706 --> 04:13.049
First, three Resource Banks: Academic and
Professional, Project and Career, Personal and
Ideas.

04:13.435 --> 04:15.932
Open any of them and just look around.

04:16.318 --> 04:17.755
You don't have to pick one.

04:18.020 --> 04:19.877
Save whatever catches your eye.

04:20.423 --> 04:21.779
We'll come back to this.

04:24.185 --> 04:27.203
Right below, there's that fake website from the
start.

04:27.589 --> 04:29.565
This is what I call zero to one.

04:29.831 --> 04:34.970
When AI starts from nothing, it has to make a
hundred little decisions on its own:

04:35.296 --> 04:38.294
the layout, the style, what to say about you.

04:38.639 --> 04:41.076
And it usually picks the most familiar thing.

04:41.522 --> 04:42.290
A gradient.

04:42.290 --> 04:43.238
Rounded cards.

04:43.544 --> 04:44.800
Big made-up numbers.

04:46.966 --> 04:50.326
Now look at the panel next to it: one to two.

04:50.326 --> 04:53.647
Here, AI starts from something that already works.

04:53.952 --> 04:55.809
It doesn't have to invent everything.

04:56.095 --> 04:57.110
It just adapts.

04:57.336 --> 04:59.373
And AI is so much better at that.

04:59.558 --> 05:01.515
That's really the main idea of today.

05:02.020 --> 05:05.221
AI is stronger at one to two than zero to one.

05:05.221 --> 05:06.480
So let's give it a one.

05:08.705 --> 05:14.046
This link opens our starting project, the Minimal
Mistakes GitHub Pages Starter.

05:14.471 --> 05:16.548
Don't worry if GitHub is new to you.

05:16.814 --> 05:18.811
The live website is the cake.

05:19.096 --> 05:20.953
This page is the recipe box.

05:21.399 --> 05:23.375
You only need three questions here.

05:23.861 --> 05:25.618
One: what does it look like?

05:25.983 --> 05:27.620
There's usually a demo link.

05:29.225 --> 05:31.626
Two: am I allowed to use it?

05:31.992 --> 05:33.109
Check the license.

05:33.496 --> 05:36.859
Just because code is public doesn't mean you can
copy it.

05:39.346 --> 05:41.383
Three: can AI read it?

05:41.668 --> 05:42.907
See all these folders?

05:42.907 --> 05:44.366
You don't need to understand them.

05:44.431 --> 05:45.367
Your Builder can.

05:45.813 --> 05:46.929
That's the whole point.

05:47.215 --> 05:50.393
It reads the real project instead of guessing from a
picture.

05:51.059 --> 05:52.716
Oh, and this list of commits?

05:53.081 --> 05:55.078
A commit is just a saved change.

05:55.224 --> 05:56.199
Remember that word.

05:57.396 --> 05:58.932
It's coming back in a minute.

05:59.238 --> 06:02.937
You don't need to click Use this template or set
anything up.

06:03.402 --> 06:06.641
The Prompt you'll copy later already has this link
in it.

06:06.966 --> 06:12.427
And if GitHub words still feel fuzzy, there's
Optional GitHub definitions on the board,

06:12.813 --> 06:14.489
right under the Resource Banks.

06:16.636 --> 06:17.793
Back on the board.

06:18.340 --> 06:21.403
Here's something a real template gives you for free.

06:21.970 --> 06:23.421
This is my Version 1.

06:23.421 --> 06:24.070
Desktop.

06:25.816 --> 06:26.673
Now phone.

06:27.020 --> 06:29.381
Same content, rearranged to fit.

06:29.728 --> 06:30.919
I didn't design that.

06:30.919 --> 06:31.949
The template did.

06:34.316 --> 06:36.554
One more idea before we move on.

06:36.980 --> 06:39.798
A reference and a template aren't the same thing.

06:40.244 --> 06:42.602
A reference shows AI what you like.

06:42.968 --> 06:45.887
A template gives AI a real project to build in.

06:46.252 --> 06:48.070
You'll have one of each today.

06:50.036 --> 06:50.512
Okay.

06:50.979 --> 06:55.689
So how did my plain 2023 site turn into what I have
now?

06:57.695 --> 06:59.472
Not with one great prompt.

06:59.858 --> 07:01.835
Every little square here is a day.

07:02.220 --> 07:04.798
Every coloured one is a day I saved changes.

07:05.223 --> 07:11.464
That's more than 379 commits, from August 2023 to
August 2026.

07:11.990 --> 07:14.207
A website really isn't one prompt.

07:14.473 --> 07:16.930
It's lots of small rounds on the same project.

07:19.236 --> 07:22.813
When I look back, those rounds fall into four
layers.

07:23.218 --> 07:25.194
Structure decides where things go.

07:25.700 --> 07:27.856
That was my 2023 template.

07:28.361 --> 07:30.718
Content decides what those places mean.

07:31.163 --> 07:33.419
That came in October 2025.

07:33.805 --> 07:36.401
Visual design is what people notice and feel.

07:37.067 --> 07:38.903
March to June 2026.

07:39.428 --> 07:44.587
And Interaction, what visitors can actually do,
started in August 2026.

07:45.392 --> 07:50.130
If you're curious, Open all six versions shows every
archived version of the site.

07:52.036 --> 07:54.713
Today we only do layer one, Structure.

07:55.059 --> 07:58.497
That might sound small, but it's the one everything
else sits on.

07:58.943 --> 08:01.881
If the structure's off, every later round has to
fight it.

08:02.186 --> 08:03.262
And here's a bonus.

08:03.448 --> 08:07.247
Once you know the four layers, you can look at any
website and ask:

08:07.332 --> 08:09.229
which layer do I actually like here?

08:09.434 --> 08:10.350
Let's try that.

08:12.276 --> 08:14.534
This part of the board has one rule:

08:15.241 --> 08:18.921
don't save a website without naming what you want to
learn from it.

08:19.547 --> 08:21.605
So let's practice with four sites.

08:21.671 --> 08:24.249
For each one, guess the layer before I tell you.

08:26.516 --> 08:27.872
Jon Barron's site.

08:28.277 --> 08:29.093
Got a guess?

08:29.318 --> 08:30.154
It's Structure.

08:30.740 --> 08:34.458
Research comes first, and every paper uses the same
pattern:

08:34.864 --> 08:38.923
an image, a title, one line about what it adds, and
links.

08:39.468 --> 08:42.746
So you wouldn't tell AI, "Copy this." You'd say,

08:42.952 --> 08:46.370
"Show my strongest work early, using one repeated
pattern."

08:48.475 --> 08:50.392
Next, Scholar in Motion.

08:50.657 --> 08:54.335
That's an open-source template I made from my own
website.

08:54.721 --> 09:00.621
It opens with a short academic story, then leads
into separate pages and a research map.

09:00.866 --> 09:03.723
Mostly Structure, with a strong Content idea.

09:04.329 --> 09:07.847
You're welcome to study or reuse the structure under
its license.

09:08.032 --> 09:11.570
Just not my words, my photos, or my research
identity.

09:13.896 --> 09:17.515
Echo Wang's portfolio looks and works like a Windows
desktop.

09:17.741 --> 09:18.439
Which layer?

09:18.439 --> 09:20.099
Interaction, and a lot of Visual.

09:20.384 --> 09:24.657
It's really clever, and the interface itself shows
how the designer thinks.

09:24.657 --> 09:25.906
But it's not for today.

09:26.512 --> 09:28.350
Save that idea for later.

09:30.596 --> 09:36.576
And Maggie Appleton's digital garden: notes, visual
essays, ideas that are still growing.

09:36.861 --> 09:38.137
You might guess Visual.

09:38.363 --> 09:40.659
But the big choice here is Structure.

09:41.045 --> 09:46.084
Instead of organizing by job or year, the site is
organized by how ideas connect.

09:46.730 --> 09:51.169
So here's a good question to borrow: if my site
isn't organized by time,

09:51.254 --> 09:52.530
what could organize it?

09:54.656 --> 09:56.413
So, four different directions.

09:56.799 --> 09:57.895
Research first.

09:58.160 --> 09:59.477
An academic story.

09:59.862 --> 10:01.799
An interface that shows a skill.

10:02.165 --> 10:03.502
A garden of ideas.

10:03.787 --> 10:05.784
You're not looking for the fanciest one.

10:05.970 --> 10:10.170
You're looking for the one that fits your visitor
and the evidence you really have.

10:12.075 --> 10:14.913
Now, how do you hand a reference to AI?

10:15.238 --> 10:16.994
With a Structure Reference Note.

10:17.480 --> 10:21.598
Here's my filled-in example, using my own 2023
homepage.

10:22.104 --> 10:23.820
At the top, what the reference is.

10:24.265 --> 10:25.521
Then, Look at.

10:26.107 --> 10:30.765
I numbered five spots: the navigation, the profile
that stays put,

10:31.071 --> 10:36.370
the first-screen message, the grouped evidence, and
the path to my CV and contact.

10:37.436 --> 10:39.754
Then, what I want AI to learn:

10:40.520 --> 10:46.042
keep the identity steady while the main column goes
from one short claim to grouped proof.

10:46.649 --> 10:51.129
What I'll change: my audience, my page names, my
themes and evidence.

10:52.696 --> 10:58.857
And the boundary: don't copy my wording, my photo,
my research labels, my look,

10:59.103 --> 11:01.260
or that butterfly I added later.

11:02.086 --> 11:03.803
That last line matters most.

11:04.188 --> 11:07.407
You're not asking AI to rebuild someone else's site.

11:07.592 --> 11:10.230
You're giving it one decision and one boundary.

11:12.435 --> 11:15.373
Scroll down to 30 to 34, Use Now.

11:15.898 --> 11:22.038
Click Copy blank Structure Note and paste it
anywhere: a doc, your notes app, wherever.

11:22.623 --> 11:25.941
You'll see two more notes here, for Visual and
Interaction.

11:26.266 --> 11:27.362
Those are for later.

11:27.727 --> 11:29.944
Just jot ideas there if you have them.

11:30.569 --> 11:33.646
And if nothing jumped out at you, that's totally
fine.

11:33.732 --> 11:34.647
Skip the note.

11:35.233 --> 11:38.350
The Prompt has a sensible default structure built
in.

11:39.895 --> 11:42.957
You're done with this part when you can say three
things:

11:43.364 --> 11:48.009
what AI may learn, what you'll change, and what it
must not copy.

11:55.895 --> 11:57.994
Okay, Build the Foundation.

11:58.460 --> 12:00.438
See the six steps across the top?

12:00.804 --> 12:03.965
Copy, Read, Approve, Inspect, Repair, Save.

12:04.391 --> 12:06.109
We'll go through them in order.

12:08.495 --> 12:10.712
Step one is the Foundation Prompt.

12:10.777 --> 12:12.193
Yes, it's long.

12:12.319 --> 12:13.414
That's on purpose.

12:13.960 --> 12:18.118
In my own work, I usually send short messages and
adjust as I go.

12:18.484 --> 12:23.303
But this one is built to work across different
Builders and head off the usual mistakes.

12:23.668 --> 12:25.104
So copy it as it is.

12:25.570 --> 12:28.827
The only thing you'd ever change is the
starting-project link,

12:29.213 --> 12:32.210
and only if you picked a different licensed
template.

12:34.176 --> 12:38.273
You don't need to read every line, but here's what
it tells AI.

12:38.739 --> 12:40.795
Look before you touch anything.

12:41.041 --> 12:44.818
Read the project's instructions, license and setup
first.

12:44.963 --> 12:46.299
Stay in this project.

12:46.605 --> 12:49.762
No blank HTML page, no brand-new app.

12:50.087 --> 12:51.623
The CV is the truth.

12:51.909 --> 12:55.326
No made-up jobs, dates, awards, papers or links.

12:55.351 --> 12:56.227
Plan first.

12:56.712 --> 13:00.750
Ask at most three questions, show a Blueprint, then
stop and wait.

13:01.455 --> 13:03.191
And stop at Version 1.

13:04.037 --> 13:07.815
No fancy design, no animations, no publishing yet.

13:08.241 --> 13:13.119
The little numbered notes next to the Prompt explain
each choice, if you're curious.

13:14.125 --> 13:16.422
One note if you're on Google AI Studio.

13:16.988 --> 13:20.746
It might not be able to run this kind of project
exactly as is,

13:21.071 --> 13:22.347
so it may adapt it.

13:22.873 --> 13:26.110
That's okay, as long as it tells you what it
changed.

13:28.016 --> 13:32.536
So: click Copy complete Prompt, paste it into your
Builder,

13:32.841 --> 13:36.460
attach your CV, your photo and your note, and send.

13:37.046 --> 13:38.323
Then come right back.

13:38.628 --> 13:40.826
Don't sit there waiting for the reply.

13:41.311 --> 13:44.189
I'll show you what happens next while yours works.

13:52.396 --> 13:54.835
So while your Builder's reading the project,

13:55.301 --> 13:58.982
here's a real run I did with Codex on August 29th.

13:59.408 --> 14:03.470
Same Prompt, my public-safe CV, and one Structure
Note.

14:05.356 --> 14:09.055
First thing it did: it asked before it changed
anything.

14:09.541 --> 14:11.779
Three questions, one at a time.

14:12.245 --> 14:13.621
Who's the first visitor?

14:14.087 --> 14:17.366
I said research collaborators and academic peers.

14:17.632 --> 14:19.609
What should they get in five seconds?

14:20.726 --> 14:27.067
Belle Li studies authentic learning design across
AI, XR, and mobile language learning.

14:27.773 --> 14:29.409
What evidence comes first?

14:29.955 --> 14:32.432
Selected publications and current studies.

14:32.838 --> 14:34.454
If you're not sure how to answer,

14:34.500 --> 14:37.097
just ask for two or three options and pick one.

14:37.683 --> 14:40.000
Just don't let AI decide who you are.

14:42.086 --> 14:45.204
Then it showed me a Structure Blueprint and stopped.

14:45.830 --> 14:47.426
This is your moment to check.

14:47.712 --> 14:48.790
Is the visitor right?

14:48.790 --> 14:50.109
The five-second message?

14:50.354 --> 14:51.791
The pages and the menu?

14:52.236 --> 14:53.895
The order on the homepage?

14:53.895 --> 14:55.727
And look at what's been postponed.

14:55.727 --> 15:00.640
Design polish, interaction, publishing: all of that
should be waiting for later.

15:02.666 --> 15:07.750
If it all looks true, go to step three and click
Copy approval sentence.

15:08.036 --> 15:09.874
Send it exactly as it is.

15:10.220 --> 15:12.800
Think of it as the key that unlocks the build.

15:14.806 --> 15:16.201
Now, my favourite part.

15:16.667 --> 15:18.763
My first build actually failed.

15:19.249 --> 15:23.506
My computer had the wrong version of Ruby, and the
theme wouldn't download.

15:23.972 --> 15:28.009
A careless agent might've thrown everything out and
made a fresh page.

15:28.475 --> 15:29.510
Codex didn't.

15:29.876 --> 15:33.133
It kept the project, used the Jekyll I already had,

15:33.398 --> 15:36.795
copied the licensed theme in, and built it properly.

15:37.161 --> 15:40.218
So remember: an error is normal, and you can fix it.

15:40.603 --> 15:43.500
What's not okay is AI replacing your project.

15:45.025 --> 15:47.205
And here's the Version 1 that came out.

15:47.672 --> 15:49.110
Yours will look different.

15:49.778 --> 15:52.559
Compare it to your Blueprint, not to my screen.

16:00.726 --> 16:01.602
Got a preview?

16:02.008 --> 16:02.503
Nice.

16:02.789 --> 16:04.786
Step four: inspect it.

16:05.232 --> 16:10.074
Look at the actual website, not just the list of
files AI says it changed.

16:10.439 --> 16:14.300
Check it wide, like a laptop, and narrow, like a
phone.

16:16.185 --> 16:17.362
Start with facts.

16:17.628 --> 16:18.665
Is your name right?

16:18.930 --> 16:22.431
Are the jobs, dates, projects and links all really
in your CV?

16:22.918 --> 16:24.355
Anything private showing?

16:24.581 --> 16:27.040
AI can slip up, even with a careful Prompt.

16:28.565 --> 16:29.522
Then structure.

16:30.008 --> 16:32.006
Does the menu match your Blueprint?

16:32.392 --> 16:34.511
Can you find your main message fast?

16:34.797 --> 16:36.535
Are the sections in the right order?

16:36.820 --> 16:39.380
Does the contact link work, and is it safe?

16:41.526 --> 16:42.341
Something off?

16:42.827 --> 16:43.762
Step five.

16:44.568 --> 16:48.106
Find the card that matches what you see and open its
fix.

16:48.772 --> 16:51.409
Did AI build before showing a Blueprint?

16:51.935 --> 16:53.090
Is the preview blank?

16:53.456 --> 16:56.233
Did it make something up, or show something it
shouldn't?

16:56.538 --> 16:59.536
Did it swap your project for a plain HTML page?

17:00.021 --> 17:01.330
There's a fix for each.

17:01.330 --> 17:04.440
Just send one fix at a time, not all four at once.

17:06.386 --> 17:10.872
Step six tells you when you're done: you can see a
true Version 1,

17:11.238 --> 17:13.879
and your Builder stopped before the next round.

17:16.186 --> 17:18.296
Last folder: Check and Save.

17:18.296 --> 17:19.484
Three quick checks.

17:19.810 --> 17:20.866
Is your name right?

17:21.131 --> 17:22.608
Are your main facts right?

17:22.893 --> 17:26.572
And do you know where this project lives and how to
open it again?

17:27.038 --> 17:28.734
Keep it in the Builder you used.

17:28.960 --> 17:32.098
A screenshot or a chat summary isn't the project.

17:32.384 --> 17:33.780
And don't start a new one.

17:35.946 --> 17:37.742
So look at what you just did.

17:38.288 --> 17:40.525
You didn't ask AI to invent you.

17:41.151 --> 17:46.611
You picked a real starting project, gave it your
real facts and a clear boundary,

17:47.157 --> 17:50.135
approved the plan, and checked what you kept.

17:50.761 --> 17:55.140
That habit works for any AI project, not just
websites.

17:57.226 --> 18:02.647
On my website, I say it in six words: AI in
Learning, Humans in Charge.

18:03.012 --> 18:06.171
The board stays open for you, so come back anytime.

18:06.676 --> 18:10.355
And if you've got a minute, there's a short survey
in the first folder.

18:10.621 --> 18:12.317
Tell me what would help you next.

18:12.863 --> 18:14.560
Thanks for building with me.
